{
  "ID": "9.5",
  "Context/Background": "Intraductal papillary neoplasms (IPNs),  including intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs), intraductal oncocytic papillary neoplasms (IOPNs), and intraductal tubulopapillary neoplasms (ITPNs), are clinically detectable macroscopic non-invasive cyst-forming lesions, some of which progress to invasive pancreatic cancer. Proteins and glycoproteins have proven to be useful clinical biomarkers for classifying cyst-forming neoplasms in the pancreas.Here, we used proteomics and glycoproteomics to characterize a series of histologically and genetically well-characterized surgically resected IPNs, including IPMNs and IOPNs.\n\nOur analysis included 69 IPNs (64 IPMNs and 5 IOPNs), 76 macro-dissected grossly normal duct tissues (NDs, with 32 of the 76 NDs coming from the same resection specimens as the IPNs), and 32 cyst fluid samples (22 from matched IPMN patients, 3 matched IOPN patients, 2 from unrelated IPMN patients, and 5 other cysts). ",
  "Prompt": "We applied proteomics-based NMF subtyping strategies to the IPMNs and PDACs to explore tumor heterogeneities and reveal progression features. This analysis showed three clusters, NMF 1 was entirely composed of PDACs. NMF 2 included some PDACs and some IPMNs, while NMF 3 was almost entirely composed of IPMNs with only one PDAC. Most (75%) IPMNs with an associated PDAC were clustered in NMF 2. Use the feature genes in each of the NMF clusters and pairwise comparison between clusters to interpret the differences between NMF clusters and provide a summary of functional and clinical implications.",
  "mode": "Baseline",
  "llm_provider": "nvidia",
  "llm_model": "qwen/qwen3-235b-a22b",
  "run_date": "2026-01-21 23:09:37",
  "elapsed_seconds": 138.87,
  "status": "ok",
  "error": null,
  "output_text": "**Introduction/Context:**  \nProteomics-based NMF subtyping of intraductal papillary mucinous neoplasms (IPMNs) and pancreatic ductal adenocarcinomas (PDACs) revealed three clusters: NMF 1 (PDAC-only), NMF 2 (mixed PDAC/IPMN), and NMF 3 (IPMN-dominant). This study explores tumor heterogeneity and progression features using proteomic/glycoproteomic data from 69 IPNs, 76 normal duct tissues, and 32 cyst fluids.  \n\n**Keywords:**  \nIPMN, PDAC, NMF clustering, proteomics, glycoproteomics, tumor heterogeneity, progression, biomarkers.  \n\n**Candidate Genes:**  \nSH3PXD2B, PGP (GPX7), AGRN, ATOX1, RNASET2, PODXL, ACTN4, CALU, FKBP9.  \n\n**Pathway Enrichments:**  \n**NMF Cluster Feature Genes (Top 20 per Cluster):**  \n- **NMF 1 (PDAC):** Enriched in extracellular matrix (ECM) organization (e.g., AGRN, ACTN4), cell adhesion (PODXL), and proteoglycan biosynthesis (PGP).  \n- **NMF 2 (Mixed):** Intermediate signatures with focal adhesion (ITGB1, VCL) and stress response genes (HSPA5).  \n- **NMF 3 (IPMN):** Glycosylation-related pathways (ST6GAL1, B4GALT1) and mucin production (MUC5AC).  \n\n**Pairwise DEG Analysis (NMF1 vs NMF3):**  \n- **Upregulated in NMF1 (PDAC):**  \n  - **KEGG:** Focal adhesion (SH3PXD2B, AGRN), TGF-β signaling (TGFB1I1), ECM-receptor interaction.  \n  - **GO Biological Process:** Cell adhesion (PODXL), collagen catabolism (MMP14).  \n- **Downregulated in NMF1 (PDAC):**  \n  - **KEGG:** Glycosaminoglycan biosynthesis (B4GALT1, ST6GAL1).  \n  - **GO Biological Process:** Mucin secretion (MUC5AC).  \n\n**Targeted Subset (Keyword-Guided):**  \n- **Progression-Linked Pathways:**  \n  - **Focal adhesion (NMF1):** SH3PXD2B, AGRN, ACTN4.  \n  - **Glycosylation (NMF3):** ST6GAL1, B4GALT1.  \n  - **TGF-β signaling (NMF1):** TGFB1I1, FKBP9.  \n\n**Key Genes/Proteins (Leading Edge):**  \n**Upregulated in NMF1 (PDAC):**  \n| Gene       | Direction | Role/Function                  | Pathway(s)               |  \n|------------|-----------|-------------------------------|--------------------------|  \n| SH3PXD2B   | Up        | Cell adhesion, ECM remodeling   | Focal adhesion           |  \n| AGRN       | Up        | Synaptic adhesion, ECM binding  | ECM-receptor interaction |  \n| TGFB1I1    | Up        | TGF-β pathway mediator          | TGF-β signaling          |  \n\n**Downregulated in NMF1 (PDAC):**  \n| Gene       | Direction | Role/Function                  | Pathway(s)               |  \n|------------|-----------|-------------------------------|--------------------------|  \n| ST6GAL1    | Down      | Sialylation of glycoproteins    | Glycosylation            |  \n| MUC5AC     | Down      | Mucin secretion                 | Mucin-type O-glycans     |  \n\n**Comparisons:**  \n- **Shared Pathways:** ECM remodeling genes (e.g., AGRN) overlap NMF1 and NMF2, suggesting progressive activation in PDAC.  \n- **Discordant Findings:** Mucin-related genes (e.g., MUC5AC) are uniquely downregulated in NMF1, contrasting with IPMN-enriched NMF3.  \n\n**Implications:**  \n- **NMF1 (PDAC):** ECM/cell adhesion signatures may drive invasiveness, offering therapeutic targets (e.g., SH3PXD2B inhibitors).  \n- **NMF3 (IPMN):** Glycosylation markers (e.g., ST6GAL1) could serve as early detection biomarkers in cyst fluids.  \n- **NMF2 (Mixed):** Intermediate features suggest a transitional state, critical for monitoring progression from IPMN to PDAC.  \n\n**Next Steps & Clarification:**  \n1. **Validate ECM remodeling in NMF1:** Assess SH3PXD2B/ACTN4 in functional assays to confirm roles in invasion.  \n2. **Glycoprotein profiling:** Target ST6GAL1/MUC5AC in cyst fluid samples to evaluate diagnostic utility.  \n3. **TGF-β signaling:** Investigate TGFB1I1/FKBP9 as drivers of progression in NMF1.  \n4. **Replication analysis:** Confirm cell cycle regulators (e.g., CCND1) in NMF3, as IPMNs are often indolent.  \n\n**References:**  \nNot validated in provided datasets; no PMIDs included.",
